.101961 .541176 .490196 RG
1.5 w
n 56.69291 748.3465 m 538.5827 748.3465 l S
BT /F2 9 Tf 10.8 TL ET
.101961 .541176 .490196 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 705.8268 Tm (Heater \(Pin 4 & 5\)) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 691.6535 Tm (A thin filament inside the tube, like a light bulb element. Put voltage across these pins and the cathode heats up so) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 680.315 Tm (it can emit electrons. Takes 10-15 seconds to warm up.) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 9 Tf 10.8 TL ET
.101961 .541176 .490196 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 654.8031 Tm (Cathode \(Pin 2\)) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 640.6299 Tm (The electron source. When heated by the filament, it emits electrons toward the target. Connected directly to GND.) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 9 Tf 10.8 TL ET
.101961 .541176 .490196 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 615.1181 Tm (Target Anode \(Pin 9\)) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 600.9449 Tm (The fluorescent screen that glows green. Electrons from the cathode hit this surface. Gets HV directly. The shadow) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 589.6063 Tm (appears where electrons are deflected away.) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 9 Tf 10.8 TL ET
.101961 .541176 .490196 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 564.0945 Tm (Grid \(Pin 1\)) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 549.9213 Tm (The control input. A small voltage change here \(0V to -14V\) controls how much the triode conducts. This is where) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 538.5827 Tm (the ESP32 PWM signal controls the shadow.) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 9 Tf 10.8 TL ET
.101961 .541176 .490196 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 513.0709 Tm (Triode Anode \(Pin 3 & Pin 7\)) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 498.8976 Tm (The amplifier output. Connected to HV through a 200k load resistor. The voltage here determines the shadow) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 487.5591 Tm (position on the display.) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 13.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 462.0472 Tm (How Grid Self-Bias Works) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 442.2047 Tm (The grid \(Pin 1\) is connected to GND through a large resistor \(1M or more\).) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 430.8661 Tm (Inside the tube, some electrons from the hot cathode land on the grid. This tiny) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 419.5276 Tm (current flows through the resistor to ground. By Ohm's law, even a few microamps) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 408.189 Tm (through 1M creates several volts of drop, making the grid negative relative to) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 396.8504 Tm (ground. This is called 'grid leak bias' and it happens naturally.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 385.5118 Tm  T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 374.1732 Tm (When Q2 \(MOSFET\) turns on, it shorts Pin 1 directly to GND, overriding the) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 362.8346 Tm (self-bias and forcing the grid to 0V. The PWM duty cycle controls how much) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 351.4961 Tm (time Q2 spends on vs off, effectively varying the average grid voltage.) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 13.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 323.1496 Tm (Shadow Behavior) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.101961 .541176 .490196 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 300.4724 Tm (PWM low \(0%\)) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 9.6 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 164.4094 300.4724 Tm (-> Q2 off -> Grid self-biases negative) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 164.4094 287.7165 Tm (-> Shadow closes -> MORE green glow = TUNED) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.101961 .541176 .490196 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 265.0394 Tm (PWM high \(100%\)) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 9.6 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 164.4094 265.0394 Tm (-> Q2 on -> Grid clamped to 0V) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 164.4094 252.2835 Tm (-> Shadow opens -> LESS green glow = BETWEEN) Tj T* ET
BT /F2 8.5 Tf 10.2 TL ET
.121569 .160784 .215686 rg
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 229.6063 Tm (Note on bias resistor value:) Tj T* ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 9.6 TL ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 215.4331 Tm (Start with 1M. If shadow range is too small, add more 1M resistors in series.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 70.86614 202.6772 Tm (2-5M gives more range. Larger resistor = more negative bias = wider shadow sweep.) Tj T* ET
 

